AUDIO: 'dive into all the nitty-gritty details of what&nbsp; these include, but just know that I spent about&nbsp;&nbsp; an hour and a half measuring, you know, all of the&nbsp; specifications for all of these different cameras&nbsp;&nbsp; and I put it all into an Excel file that I will&nbsp; put on the screen now. Um, I even included things&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in there like the length of the power cable,&nbsp; length of the cable going to the rear camera,&nbsp;&nbsp; um, all sorts of information. So hopefully that\'ll&nbsp; do a better job than me trying to explain that for&nbsp;&nbsp; you know every single one of these. Um few other&nbsp; things worth noting here. Um as I was diving&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into the specifications like I was saying I found&nbsp; something out that was kind of unique. So first of&nbsp;&nbsp; all if you look at a lot of these different dash&nbsp; cameras they all claim to have some type of Sony&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'starvis sensor. And it really didn\'t matter what&nbsp; Thinkware model you look at or what VFO model you&nbsp;&nbsp; look at or even I believe the Rove also claims one&nbsp; of these starvis sensors. And I\'m looking at this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going, well, how can a $100 Q200 dash cam from&nbsp; Thinkware have the same sensor as a U3000 that\'s,&nbsp;&nbsp; you know, $450 or $500? How How is this even&nbsp; possible? Well, as it turns out, Sony Starvis is a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'line of sensors made by Sony. So, to put this into&nbsp; automotive context, it\'s like saying your vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp; has an LS engine in it. So, yeah, you could have a&nbsp; 4.8 L truck motor in your car, or you could have a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '638 horsepower LS9. Um, yes, they\'re are both LS&nbsp; engines, but they\'re pretty far apart, and these&nbsp;&nbsp; sensors are kind of the same way. One other&nbsp; final thing worth noting here is some of what&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they claim on these packages and in the marketing&nbsp; materials you can\'t actually achieve with any of&nbsp;&nbsp; these cameras. So here is what I\'m saying. So take&nbsp; this VFO A329 down here on the end for example. So&nbsp;&nbsp; this is a 4K 60 frame per second camera. Well,&nbsp; this camera will not do 4K 60 frames per second&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with HDR turned on. If you turn off HDR, yes,&nbsp; it will do that. Um, so even like this 229 Pro,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can get 60 frames per second out of a 229 Pro,&nbsp; but it\'s not going to be at 4K. But when you look&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at the packages on a lot of these, they will claim&nbsp; both. They\'ll literally say 4K 60 frames a second,&nbsp;&nbsp; even though the camera can\'t actually deliver 4K&nbsp; 60 frames per second. So hopefully all that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sense. So, there\'s certain things about doing&nbsp; these type of tests that just put a smile on my&nbsp;&nbsp; face, and I just want to share this with you guys.&nbsp; Take a look at the inside of this vehicle. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s 12 dash cameras attached to the dash. Look&nbsp; at the wiring in the passenger seat. Um, they wire&nbsp;&nbsp; into the two cigarette lighters. There\'s, you&nbsp; know, one there in the console and then another&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one here inside. And then back in the back, we\'ve&nbsp; got seven dash cameras back there. May God have&nbsp;&nbsp; mercy on the soul of the person that runs into me&nbsp; doing this testing. So, as you guys saw there, my&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'install was not the cleanest thing in the world.&nbsp; As you would imagine, when you try to strap 12&nbsp;&nbsp; dash cameras to one windshield, um there\'s just no&nbsp; neat and tidy way to do that. Um but I thought you&nbsp;&nbsp; guys would get a kick out of that. I know I did.&nbsp; Every time I opened the door, I was it just put&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a smile on my face. Anyway, what you guys need to&nbsp; understand about this video is because I\'m dealing&nbsp;&nbsp; with 12 different dash cameras, a simple 10-minute&nbsp; test drive generates 2 hours worth of video. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; what I\'m going to do is I\'m going to put these&nbsp; dash cameras into scenarios that I feel is it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is going to be relevant to the performance so you&nbsp; guys can, you know, make an educated decision as&nbsp;&nbsp; what\'s good and what\'s bad. Um, one other thing&nbsp; I want to mention here is I\'m not going to throw&nbsp;&nbsp; shade at anybody with what I\'m about to say but&nbsp; this video that you\'re watching is 4K 60 frames&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'per second. That is how I upload to YouTube.&nbsp; Well, if you\'re a content creator who is creating&nbsp;&nbsp; content on dash cams and you\'re trying to actually&nbsp; put images from the dash cams on the screen, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if that dash cam is 4K 60 frames a second and you&nbsp; don\'t upload to YouTube in 4K 60 frames a second,&nbsp;&nbsp; what did you just show the audience? See what&nbsp; I\'m saying? So, the resolution of this video&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you\'re watching is as good or possibly&nbsp; better than some of these dash cameras. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; just keep that in mind. So, the first test that&nbsp; we\'re going to do here is what I\'m going to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m just simply going to go for a drive and&nbsp; I\'m going to tell you guys a story of what&nbsp;&nbsp; ultimately spurred this video. And as I\'m driving&nbsp; along, I\'m going to cut from camera to camera to&nbsp;&nbsp; camera to camera and write down the list. And&nbsp; what that\'s going to do for you guys is that\'s&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first of all going to allow you to judge the audio&nbsp; quality of each one of these dash cameras because&nbsp;&nbsp; you do have the option with these dash cameras to&nbsp; record interior audio. The other thing it\'s going&nbsp;&nbsp; to do is it\'s literally going to let you see in&nbsp; the exact same environment in the same scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a backtoback comparison of each one of these dash&nbsp; cameras looking you know full field of view across&nbsp;&nbsp; the entire screen as well as the audio. What does&nbsp; it sound like? So, with that, well, let\'s jump&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into it. So, ultimately what spurred this video is&nbsp; my wife and I about a year and a half ago or so,&nbsp;&nbsp; we were involved in a fender bender. And basically&nbsp; what happened was my wife was driving. She was she&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was getting to ready to make a left-hand turn at&nbsp; a traffic light. The traffic light was red. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; we\'re sitting there stopped at the light and a&nbsp; woman behind us um basically bumped into the back&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of us at like maybe a half a mile an hour. Like it&nbsp; wasn\'t much. So it I look back and it\'s an older&nbsp;&nbsp; lady handicap sticker in the window clearly, you&nbsp; know, between late 70s, early 80s, somewhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that neighborhood. I have her pull over into a gas&nbsp; station that was right there on the corner and I&nbsp;&nbsp; said, "Hey, uh got out of the car, just made sure&nbsp; she was okay, and called the police and waited for&nbsp;&nbsp; the police to show up." In between the time that,&nbsp; you know, I had called the police and the police&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'showed up, the old lady also made a phone call&nbsp; to her daughter to let her daughter know what&nbsp;&nbsp; was going on, which is fine. Well, the daughter&nbsp; shows up before the police get there. Daughter&nbsp;&nbsp; gets in the passenger seat of the vehicle. I\'m&nbsp; sitting in the passenger seat of our vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the old lady and her daughter are to my right.&nbsp; So the cop shows up and gives us the form that you&nbsp;&nbsp; need to fill out detailing, you know, essentially&nbsp; what went on to cause these two cars to run into&nbsp;&nbsp; one another. So my wife is filling out the form&nbsp; because, well, she was driving. Well, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'car next to us, the daughter who wasn\'t there is&nbsp; telling her, I would say, slightly demented mother&nbsp;&nbsp; who doesn\'t really fully comprehend the form that&nbsp; she\'s reading how to fill it out. the daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wasn\'t there and she\'s telling her her mother how&nbsp; to fill out the form what caused her to get in&nbsp;&nbsp; the accident and I\'m sitting in the passenger seat&nbsp; with a window down listening to this because it\'s&nbsp;&nbsp; hot outside and the daughter goes the light turned&nbsp; green my wife and I took off and we stopped which&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'caused the car behind us to run into the back of&nbsp; us which was a bold-faced lie. the woman that\'s&nbsp;&nbsp; telling the the person writing this information&nbsp; wasn\'t even there. So, I call I look over. I&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'roll the window up because I tell my wife I\'m&nbsp; like, "I can\'t listen to this because I\'m going&nbsp;&nbsp; to blow up and these people if I got to listen&nbsp; to these boldfaced lies like this." So, I roll&nbsp;&nbsp; the window up and I kind of lean over and I just&nbsp; get the cop\'s attention. And I\'m like, "Hey, um,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m not trying to be a dick or a jerk or telling&nbsp; you how to do your job or anything, but you\'ve&nbsp;&nbsp; got a person in that car over there that wasn\'t&nbsp; here for the accident, and they\'re telling the&nbsp;&nbsp; person who was in the accident how to fill out the&nbsp; form." And the cop looked at me, he goes, "I get&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. Sometimes the juice isn\'t worth the squeeze,&nbsp; basically." And he goes, "It\'s not really going to&nbsp;&nbsp; matter what she writes on that form. It\'s pretty&nbsp; clear she ran into the back of you. So, no matter&nbsp;&nbsp; what she says, it\'s going to be her fault. Um,&nbsp; it\'s I think it\'s statistically it\'s like 80 or&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '99 I think statistically it\'s like a 99% certainty&nbsp; that if you run it in the back of somebody, you\'re&nbsp;&nbsp; at fault. So, that is what ultimately brought,&nbsp; you know, the idea of a dash cam to my attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Kind of got it in the front of my mind and&nbsp; that\'s ultimately why I am shooting this video.&nbsp;&nbsp; Now, the second test I want to really do here&nbsp; is going to be license plate readability. So, at&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different distances from the vehicle, I\'m going to&nbsp; hold up a license plate. And what we\'re going to&nbsp;&nbsp; do is we\'re just simply going to see at what point&nbsp; can you no longer read the license plate with&nbsp;&nbsp; each one of these cameras. Now, what you need to&nbsp; understand is this test is kind of ideal best case&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scenario for every one of these cameras because,&nbsp; well, think about it. I\'m standing stationary&nbsp;&nbsp; holding up a license plate. Well, that\'s a totally&nbsp; different scenario than a vehicle passing by you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at 60 mph headed the opposite direction. So,&nbsp; what we\'re going to go over, like I said,&nbsp;&nbsp; is best case scenario here. So, what I\'m going&nbsp; to do here is I\'m going to walk out 10 ft from&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the front of the vehicle and freeze the video. So,&nbsp; at this point now, I am holding this license plate&nbsp;&nbsp; 10 ft from the front of this car. And you can see&nbsp; pretty plainly that there\'s actually a discernable&nbsp;&nbsp; difference in resolution between each one of these&nbsp; images I have here on the screen. Now, what you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need to understand is the images that I have here&nbsp; on the bottom are the low price point products in&nbsp;&nbsp; this test. So, these are going to be around that&nbsp; $100 price point somewhere. And then what you\'re&nbsp;&nbsp; seeing here on the top is kind of that low to mid&nbsp; price point of about $200 or so. And taking a look&nbsp;&nbsp;'

