[15:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all three of these rear cameras from Thinkware are&nbsp; rated at the exact same resolution and frame rate,&nbsp;&nbsp; but you can in fact see a difference. So, let me&nbsp; take a step out here to 30 ft and pause it again&nbsp;&nbsp; and talk about the VFOs. So, the VFOs, the 229&nbsp; plus, the 229 Pro, and the 329S, all three of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these, again, just like Thinkwares, are rated at&nbsp; the exact same resolution and the exact same frame&nbsp;&nbsp; rate. And honestly, I see no difference between&nbsp; a 229 Plus and a 329s as far as the rear camera&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image quality here. Um, the only thing really&nbsp; worth mentioning about the VFO cameras is the&nbsp;&nbsp; VFO 329S is the only one in this test that uses&nbsp; a coax cable going to the rear. The rest of these&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use a USB cable. Well, a coax cable will actually&nbsp; transmit data much faster than what a USB cable&nbsp;&nbsp; will. So, in theory, you should be able to put a&nbsp; better camera on a 329S than what you can any of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the 229 series units from VFO. Does it actually&nbsp; translate into the real world? It doesn\'t appear&nbsp;&nbsp; as though it does, judging by the images I have&nbsp; here. So, just know at this point in the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I ran multiple other tests with each one of these&nbsp; dash cams, and I\'m not going to show you all of&nbsp;&nbsp; that test footage because, well, the results&nbsp; were either the same or sort of inconclusive.&nbsp;&nbsp; So, just to give you an idea of some of the other&nbsp; tests that I did, um it just so happened that my&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'local township had a radar trailer measuring&nbsp; everybody\'s speed on my test route. So, that&nbsp;&nbsp; allowed me to check the speedometer readings for&nbsp; every one of these dash cams I have behind me. And&nbsp;&nbsp; as you would imagine, each one of the dash cams&nbsp; with was within, you know, 1 to 2 m an hour of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what the township said my speed actually was. So,&nbsp; what that can allow you to do is if you\'re falsely&nbsp;&nbsp; accused of a speeding ticket, well, you can use&nbsp; your dash cam footage to prove that, well, you&nbsp;&nbsp; weren\'t in fact actually speeding. The second test&nbsp; that I ran was I also ran my test route at night&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just to see what the footage looked like from&nbsp; each one of the dash cams at night. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp; I didn\'t really see a huge change. The only thing&nbsp; that I do want to mention is the Thinkware U3000.&nbsp;&nbsp; This dash cam has a dedicated night vision mode.&nbsp; And from what I saw in that footage, well, they&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simply took the video and made it black and white.&nbsp; It didn\'t really give you any more resolution than&nbsp;&nbsp; any of the other cameras in this test. And the&nbsp; thing is about that, well, think about it. When&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re driving around at night, you\'re going to&nbsp; be using your headlights, and your headlights are&nbsp;&nbsp; going to light up the road in front of you. So,&nbsp; I could see the benefit of a night vision mode if&nbsp;&nbsp; you were heavily rellyant on a parking mode, but&nbsp; for driving down the road with your headlights on,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really doesn\'t matter that, you know, these&nbsp; cameras have a night vision function. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp; the final test that I do want to conduct in this&nbsp; video is what I\'m going to do is I\'m going to take&nbsp;&nbsp; each one of the cameras and I\'m rolling up to the&nbsp; exact same intersection at the exact same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I\'m just going to play about 15 seconds worth&nbsp; of intersection footage for you guys. And we\'re&nbsp;&nbsp; going to jump from camera to camera to camera&nbsp; showing you the exact same video feed from each&nbsp;&nbsp; camera. So that way you guys can make exact AB&nbsp; comparisons between any of the cameras that I have&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sitting on the bench behind me here. So taking a&nbsp; look at this image I have on the screen. This is&nbsp;&nbsp; going to be common for every camera in this test.&nbsp; And I\'m going have these sorted in order of price.&nbsp;&nbsp; So the first one we\'re looking at here is the VFO&nbsp; A119. This is the cheapest camera in this test at&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '$90. And I really want you to pay attention&nbsp; to, first of all, you know, how the cars move&nbsp;&nbsp; across the screen. But also pay attention to the&nbsp; brick building on the far opposite corner there,&nbsp;&nbsp; that brown brick building, that is a post office.&nbsp; And you can actually read that it is a post office&nbsp;&nbsp;'

